\nAnd e2 is occupied by a White knight (NWF1). So this is a capture. And after Nx e2, is it check? The knight on e2 attacks d4, f4, but also the White king on e1? No — Black knight on e2 would attack d4 (irrelevant), f4, g3, g1, f0… specifically: knight on e2 attacks f4, g3, g1, f0? No — standard knight moves: from e2, attacks d4, f4, d0, f0 — no.\n\nFrom e2, knight attacks:\n- d4 (occupied by my own knight? No — one knight per piece)\n- f4\n- g3\n- g1\n- d0 (invalid)\n- c3\n- c1\n\nKing is on e1. So e1 is adjacent to d1, d2, e2, f1, f2. So knight on e2 attacks f4, g3, g1, c3, c1, d4, f4 — **not** e1. So not check.\n\nBut still — exchanging a knight for a knight? I have N on d4, White has N on e2. Both developed. Is it good? Probably equal, but removes a defender.\n\nBut better: what about central control? Another idea: play P: e5-e4, kicking the knight on d4? No — e5 is my pawn, d4 is my knight. e5-e4 would push my pawn into d4? No — e5 to e4 is downward? No — ranks increase upward. Black is on top. So for Black, pawns move *down* the board.\n\nCritical: pawns move toward lower ranks for White, higher ranks for Black? No!\n\nStandard chess: White starts on ranks 1-2, moves toward 8. Black starts on 7-8, moves toward 1.\n\nSo for Black, pawns move from rank 7→6→5→4→3→2→1.\n\nSo from e5 (rank 5), a Black pawn moves to e4 (rank 4), then e3, etc.\n\nSo P: e5-e4 is a valid move — advancing toward White’s king.\n\nAnd e4 is attacked by White’s knight on d4? Currently, my knight is on d4. Wait — d4 is occupied by *my* knight (NBF1). So if I play e5-e4, I am moving my pawn to e4, which is adjacent to d4 — my own knight. No problem.\n\nBut is e4 protected? After e5-e4, the pawn on e4 could be attacked by White’s knight on f2 or other pieces. But more importantly, it controls d3 and f3, pressures d4 and f4.\n\nBut perhaps better to develop minor pieces.\n\nNow, notice: White has a rook on g5 — very advanced. How did it get there? And is it hanging?\n\nWhat attacks g5? My kni