wins material immediately.\n- **N: a1-c2** – Also attacks c2, which is defended by White’s king — less appealing unless part of a fork, but c2 is likely defended.\n- Pawn moves like **a7-a6**, **b7-b6**, etc., are slow and don’t capitalize on immediate opportunities.\n- **B: g4-f5** or **B: g4-e6** — these develop the bishop, but don’t exploit a tactical shot.\n- **R: a8-d8** — prepares centralization, but slower than winning a piece.\n\nWait — critical observation: **N: a1-b3** is a valid move and captures White’s knight on b3, which is undefended. That knight came from somewhere (likely c3 or a3) and is now hanging. This is a free piece!\n\nReason: Capturing the knight on b3 wins a full piece (knight) for no cost. Material advantage is crucial, and White cannot recapture because nothing defends the knight. This move improves development while gaining material — highest priority.\n\nAnswer: N: a1-b3"}
