g rooks or preparing to push.\n\nBut **P: e4-d3** wins material immediately — highly advantageous.\n\nAlso, **P: g7-g5** looks like a flank expansion, possibly preparing g6 and Bg7, but slow.\n\nReason: Capturing the bishop on d3 with the e4 pawn gains a free piece (assuming white doesn’t recapture effectively), significantly increasing Black’s advantage. Development is important, but when a free piece is available, it should almost always be taken unless there’s a tactical refutation. No immediate threat to the e4 pawn is seen. After ...exd3, White may recapture with cxd3 or Qxd3, but Black will have disrupted the structure and gained tempo.\n\nTherefore, **P: e4-d3** is the most compelling move.\n\nAnswer: P: e4-d3"}
