ulative sacrifice? Might disrupt White’s position, but likely loses the bishop for little compensation unless there’s a follow-up.\n- **P: f7-f5 / g7-g5 / h7-h5** – Kingside pawn storms, but these weaken the king’s position and are premature without castling or development.\n- **R: g8-e8 / R: g8-f8** – Prepares to connect rooks or support e-file action, but currently no immediate threat on e-file.\n- **K: d8-e7 / K: d8-e8** – King moving early; risky and generally bad practice unless forced.\n\nThe most promising move is **P: a7-b6**, capturing the knight on b6. It removes a key attacking piece from White, gains material (knight for pawn), and opens lines for Black’s pieces. The knight was White’s most advanced piece and a potential threat; eliminating it improves Black’s position significantly.\n\nReason: Capturing the knight on b6 is the best move because it wins material and eliminates a centralized enemy piece while improving Black’s structural coherence. It also activates the possibility of playing ...b5 or developing the queen-side naturally.\n\nAnswer: P: a7-b6"}
