\n1 |    |    |    |RWF1|    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[K: e7-d8, K: e7-e6, K: e7-f6, K: e7-f7, K: e7-f8, R: e8-d8, R: e8-c8, R: e8-b8, R: e8-a8, R: e8-f8, R: e8-g8, R: e8-h8, P: g4-g3, P: h5-h4]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': 'Situation: I am playing as Black, and my king is on e7, which is quite exposed in the center of the board. White’s king is on g2, relatively safe for now. The only active pieces are rooks and pawns. My rook on e8 is undeveloped but can be brought into play. There is a white pawn on d5 (PWF1), and black pawns on b7, b6, h5, and g4. Notably, the black pawn on g4 can advance to g3, potentially threatening promotion in a few moves. My king has limited mobility but is not currently in check.\n\nEvaluation:\n- King moves (e7-d8, e7-e6, e-f6, e7-f7, e7-f8): Moving the king toward d8 or f8 might help centralize it or get it to safer territory, but the king remains vulnerable. e7-e6 weakens the king’s position by moving toward the center without shelter. e7-f8 or e7-d8 are slightly better as they head toward corners, but still risky.\n- Rook moves (e8-d8, e8-c8, etc.): Bringing the rook to d8 supports potential pressure along the d-file, possibly targeting the white pawn on d5. However, there’s no immediate threat or capture. Still, rook activation is generally valuable.\n- P: g4-g3: This is a strong pawn push. It advances toward promotion (only 3 squares from queening) and threatens to open lines near the white king. After g3, next move could be g2 (if unopposed), forcing tactical complications. This move increases winning chances by creating a passed pawn and initiative.\n- P: h5-h4: Less urgent; it doesn’t contribute much immediately and may become isolated.\n\nReason: The key to winning is creating a passed pawn and pressuring the opponent. The g4-g3 move creates immediate threats and limits White’s ability to consolidate. It forces White to respond (likely with h2-h3 or Kxg3), whereas passive moves allow White to improve their position. Activating the rook or moving the king is safer but less dynamic. Since we must maximize winning chances, the most aggressive and promising move is g4-g3.\n\nAnswer: P: g4-g3'}
