 move is R: d8-d3, which checks the white king. After R:d3+, White must move the king (to f2, f3, d2, d3? — but d3 is occupied). Let’s see: Ke3-f2 or Ke3-f3 or Ke3-d2 are possible, but all are awkward and lead to loss of material or further attack.\n\nReason: Delivering check with the rook maximizes pressure, exploits the exposed white king, and likely wins material or leads to a quick mating attack. It is the most aggressive and highest-percentage winning move.\n\nAnswer: R: d8-d3"}
