\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[R: b8-a8, R: b8-c8, R: b8-d8, R: b8-e8, R: b8-f8, R: b8-g8, R: b8-h8, K: c7-c8, K: c7-d6, K: c7-d7, K: c7-d8, P: h3-h2]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is highly simplified with very few pieces remaining. My king is on c7, my rook on b8, and a pawn on h3. The enemy white king is on g6, and there's a white pawn on b5. White appears to have just moved the pawn from b7 to b5 (since it's marked PWF1, meaning it has moved, but not two squares), so no en passant is available. My king is in danger of being boxed in, and I must act carefully.\n\nMy main threats are:\n- The White king is relatively exposed near the center.\n- My rook on b8 is undeveloped and can become active.\n- My king needs to avoid being trapped or checkmated.\n\nCurrently, the Black king on c7 is under potential pressure. If it doesn't move carefully, it could be driven into a corner or lose the ability to make progress. The rook on b8 is currently passive and needs activation. The pawn on h3 is close to queening but is blocked only by lack of moves — it can go h3-h2 next.\n\nEvaluation:\nLet’s evaluate each valid move:\n\n1. Rook moves (b8-a8, b8-c8, b8-d8, b8-e8, b8-f8, b8-g8, b8-h8):\n   - All these aim to activate the rook along the 8th rank.\n   - However, the 8th rank is empty except for corners. Placing the rook on h8 might support future attack or control of the h-file, especially since my pawn is on h3 and heading toward h2, potentially h1.\n   - b8-h8: Maximizes rook mobility along the h-file, supporting the h-pawn’s promotion. This is strategically strong.\n\n2. King moves:\n   - c7-c8: Centralizes the king slightly, but c8 is less active than d7 or d6.\n   - c7-d6: Moves toward the center and closer to the action. Also avoids being stuck on the back rank. Could support a central push or coordination with rook.\n   - c7-d7: Similar, but less aggressive than d6.\n   - c7-d8: Retreats further from action; not ideal.\n   - c7-d8 and c7-d7 are safer but passive.\n\n3. Pawn move h3-h2:\n   - Advances the h-pawn one step from h3 to h2.\n   - Next move could be h2-h1=Q, queening the pawn.\n   - This is a critical threat: if unopposed, promoting the h-pawn will likely win the game.\n   - White king on g6 is close and could potentially intercept if not careful, but it's still two moves away unless Black delays.\n\nKey insight: The fastest path to victory is promoting the h-pawn. Every move that doesn’t promote or support this risks allowing White time to maneuver the king to block or capture.\n\nh3-h2 brings me one step from queening. After h2-h1=Q, I will have a queen and rook vs king, which is an easy win.\n\nCompare:\n- Rook moves: Delay promotion, give White time to respond.\n- King moves: Also delay the pawn advance — dangerous when promotion is so close.\n- h3-h2: Forces the issue, threatens immediate queening.\n\nReason: The most forcing and winning move is to push the h-pawn. With h3-h2, I threaten h2-h1=Q on the next move. White cannot stop both the promotion and deal with the rook or king activity. Delaying this pawn move risks allowing the White king to approach (e.g., Kg6-f5 or Ke5) and potentially stop the promotion. Therefore, pushing the pawn now maximizes winning chances.\n\nAnswer: P: h3-h2"}
