 if supported — but currently no piece supports that.\n- R: b7-f7 – This attacks the square g6 directly! Rf7+ would give check to the White king on g6. That is a forcing move.\n- R: b7-g7 – Attacks the king directly? Wait — g7 is adjacent to g6. So Rg7+ would also be a check!\n- R: b7-h7 – Also attacks h7, but less relevant.\n\nWait — let's clarify:\n\n- White king is on g6.\n- Rook moves:\n  - R: b7-g7 → rook goes to g7, which is same file, one rank above g6 → so yes, **Rg7+** gives check, attacking the king vertically.\n  - R: b7-f7 → rook to f7, same rank as g6? No: g6 is on rank 6, f7 is on rank 7 → so f7 is one file left and one rank up from g6 → not aligned. So Rf7 does **not** attack g6.\n  - But g7 is on the same file as g6 → so Rg7+ **does** attack the king.\n\nSo among the valid moves, **R: b7-g7** is a check.\n\nSimilarly:\n- R: b7-h7 → h7 is adjacent to g6 diagonally, but rook doesn't move diagonally → h7 does not attack g6.\n\nTherefore, only **R: b7-g7** delivers check.\n\nNow, what happens after **Rg7+**?\n\n- White king on g6 is checked by rook on g7.\n- King must move: possible escapes:\n  - g6-f6\n  - g6-h6\n  - g6-f7\n  - g6-h7\nBut f7, h7 are empty; f6 and h6 are empty too. All seem possible unless blocked.\n\nBut critically: **this is a discovered opportunity** — delivering check with the rook may force the king into a worse position.\n\nMoreover, after Rg7+, if White moves Kg8? No — g8 is occupied? Let's see: current board shows d8 has black king, g8 is empty? Yes, g8 is empty.\n\nWait — Black king is on d8, g8 is free. So White king could go to g8? Only if coming from f7 or h7, but from g6, it can go to g7? No — g7 will be occupied by the rook.\n\nAfter Rb7-g7, rook is on g7, so g7 is occupied — so White king cannot go to g7.\n\nAvailable escape squares for White king (from g6): f6, f7, h6, h7, g7 — but g7 is attacked and now occupied by rook → so g7 is blocked.\n\nSo legal escapes: f6, f7, h6, h7.\n\nAll of these are on the edge. We can potentially chase it.\n\nBut more importantly: is there a follow-up?\n\nSuppose we play Rg7+, forcing Kf6 — then we can play Kb8-c7 or something to centralize, but better: Rh7+ or something? But we only have one rook.\n\nAlternatively, consider **king movement**: Kd8-e7 — brings our king closer to center, but not urgent.\n\nBut **Rg7+** is the only forcing move available — it gains tempo and exploits the poor placement of White’s king.\n\nOther rook moves don’t challenge the king at all.\n\nAlso, moving pawns like b5-b4 or c6-c5 does nothing useful — they’re slow and don’t threaten anything.\n\nKing moves like Kd8-e7 might be good later, but right now, we have a chance to **check the enemy king** — and we should take it, especially when it’s so poorly placed.\n\nReason: R: b7-g7 is the only move that delivers check, immediately punishing the overextended White king. It forces White into a defensive posture, limits king mobility, and creates opportunities for further attacks. Even if it doesn’t lead directly to mate, it maximizes pressure and chances to win.\n\nAnswer: R: b7-g7"}
