\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b5-b4, P: c6-c5, K: d8-c7, K: d8-c8, K: d8-d7, K: d8-e7, K: d8-e8, R: g7-f7, R: g7-e7, R: g7-d7, R: g7-c7, R: g7-b7, R: g7-a7, R: g7-g6, R: g7-g5, R: g7-g4, R: g7-g3, R: g7-g2, R: g7-g1, R: g7-g8, R: g7-h7]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I am playing as Black, and the board is highly simplified with only a few pieces remaining. My king (KBF1) is on d8, my rook (RBF1) is on g7, and two pawns on b5 and c6. White has a king (KWF1) on f5 and a pawn on f5 as well. The position appears to be in the endgame phase, with both sides having minimal material. White’s king is active and central, currently on f5, and could threaten my pawns or aim to promote its own. My rook is on an open file and semi-open rank, potentially useful for checking or restricting the white king. My king is somewhat passive but not in immediate danger.\n\nKey observations:\n- The black pawn on c6 is one square away from promoting if supported, but it's blocked by space and the white king is nearby.\n- The pawn on b5 is ready to advance.\n- The rook on g7 can exert pressure along the 7th rank or support potential checks.\n- White’s king on f5 is close to entering a favorable position—possibly targeting my pawns or supporting its own if any exist (though none are visible now).\n- My king on d8 needs activation but must avoid walking into checks or losing material.\n- Immediate threats: No direct check, but White could play Ke6 or Kd6 next, threatening invasion.\n\nEvaluation of promising moves:\n\n1. P: b5-b4 – Advances the pawn, but doesn’t gain much space or threat; may become weak later. Slow and passive.\n2. P: c6-c5 – Opens the diagonal for king activity, controls d4, but blocks the c-file and doesn’t directly push toward promotion. Could be useful for coordination.\n3. K: d8-c7 – Centralizes the king, supports the c6 pawn, prepares to escort it forward. Very useful in endgames to activate the king.\n4. K: d8-e7 – Also brings king toward center and closer to the kingside, but less connected to c6 pawn.\n5. R: g7-g8 – Places rook behind the black king? Not useful. Or possibly preparing support, but no immediate benefit.\n6. R: g7-g1 – Rook going to g1 seems pointless—far from action.\n7. R: g7-a7 – Long-range move across the 7th rank. This attacks the entire a7-h7 line. If White’s king stays on f5, this could restrict Ke6 or Kd6 by attacking e7, d7, etc. More importantly, **this move puts pressure on the 7th rank**, which is powerful in endgames. It limits White’s king mobility and prepares for potential skewers or checks.\n\nMost promising options:\n- K: d8-c7 — Activates king toward the center and supports the c6 pawn’s advance. Critical in king and pawn endgames.\n- R: g7-a7 — Maximizes rook activity by controlling the 7th rank, restricting White’s king (e.g., after Ra7, Ke6 is met with ...Ra6+), and preparing to support future operations.\n\nHowever, since I have a rook, using it actively while centralizing the king is ideal. But note: **White’s king is on f5**, and if I play Kc7, White might respond with Ke6, threatening to penetrate further.\n\nBut more urgent: Can I create a threat?\n\nWait — what about **R: g7-g8+**? That would be a check on g8. Let’s see: g8 is empty, so moving Rg7-g8 gives **check**? Yes! Because the white king is on f5 — no, wait: g8 is not aligned with f5. g8 is h-file top, f5 is two files left and three ranks down — not same rank, file, or diagonal. So **not a check**.\n\nBut **R: g7-e7**? That would control e7 — which is adjacent to f5 (f5-e7 is knight distance), so no check either.\n\nActually, none of the rook moves give check. So quiet maneuvering.\n\nBest plan: Activate king to support pawn promotion while using rook to restrain White’s king.\n\nTop candidates:\n- **K: d8-c7** — Best king move. Supports c6 pawn, prepares c5-c4-c3 and eventual promotion. Centralization is key.\n- **R: g7-a7** — Long-term restraint. Controls 7th rank, prevents White king from safely occupying d7/e7/f7/g7/h7 without being harassed.\n\nBut **R: g7-g6**? Threatens to cut off the white king from the kingside. But f6 is occupied? No — f6 is empty. g6 is safe. But g6 doesn't block much.\n\nBetter: **R: g7-e7** — cuts off the white king from e6, e7, e8. Since e6 is a key square for invading the queenside, placing the rook on e7 **controls the entire e-file**, especially e6 and e5.\n\nAfter **R: g7-e7**, the white king cannot go to e6 — critical!\n\nSimilarly, **R: g7-d7**, **c7**, **b7**, **a7** all control those files.\n\nBut **e7** is most central and disruptive.\n\nIndeed, **R: g7-e7** immediately challenges White’s king centralization.\n\nCompare:\n- **R: g7-e7** — Controls e6, e5, e4… and supports future king marches.\n- **K: d8-c7** — Prepares to push c6-c5, then c4, etc.\n\nWhich is better?\n\nIf I don’t restrain the white king, it will go Ke6 or Kd6 and attack my pawns.\n\nSo **immediate restriction** is vital.\n\nTherefore, **R: g7-e7** is stronger than king movement because it denies White a key square *now*.\n\nAlso, after Re7, if White plays Kf6 or Kg6, I can consider ...Rg7 or counterplay.\n\nAdditionally, my king is not under threat, so I can afford to play the rook first.\n\nFinal evaluation:\n- **R: g7-e7**: Denies Ke6, increases control, activates rook optimally.\n- **K: d8-c7**: Good, but slower; White responds Ke6 and invades.\n\nThus, **rook to e7** is the most accurate and pressing move.\n\nReason: To prevent White’s king from advancing to e6 or d6, gaining control of the center and threatening my pawns. Placing the rook on the 7th rank (e7) maximizes its influence and restricts the enemy king immediately.\n\nAnswer: R: g7-e7"}
