ares apart — no\n    - **g4**: g4 is adjacent to g5 (same file, one square apart) → **Qg4+**\n    - **h5**: h5 is adjacent to g5 (same rank, one file apart) → **Qh5+**\n    - **f4**: f4 is one down and one left — diagonal? f4 to g5 is diagonal (f4–g5) → yes, adjacent diagonally → **Qf4+**\n\nSo all of these queen moves give check:\n- Q: h3-f5 → Qf5+\n- Q: h3-g4 → Qg4+\n- Q: h3-h5 → Qh5+\n- Q: h3-f4 → Qf4+\n\nNow, among these, which is best?\n\nBut wait — **Q: h3-g3** is listed, but g3 is not attacking g5 (g3 to g5 is two ranks apart). So not a check.\n\nNow, consider **Q: h3-d7** — too far.\n\nBut look: **Q: h3-h7** — h7 is on the h-file. h7 to g5? Diagonally: h7–g6–f5–e4… no, not aligned. So not attacking.\n\nWait — **Q: h3-h6**: h6 is on same file. h6 to g5? h6–g5 is diagonal (one up, one left) → yes! h6–g5 is diagonal adjacent → **Qh6+ check**!\n\nSimilarly:\n- **Q: h3-h4**: h4 to g5? h4–g5 is diagonal (up one, left one) → yes → **Qh4+ check**!\n\nSo actually, many queen moves deliver check.\n\nList of queen moves from h3 that attack g5 (i.e., check):\n\n- Horizontal: none (no same rank)\n- Vertical: g-file? No. h-file? h3 to h4, h5, h6, h7, h8 — but g5 is on g-file → so only if diagonal.\n\nDiagonal attackers to g5:\n- From f4: f4–g5 (diagonal)\n- From h4: h4–g5 (anti-diagonal)\n- From h6: h6–g5 (anti-diagonal down)\n- From f6: f6–g5 (diagonal)\n- From g4: g4–g5 (vertical)\n- From h5: h5–g5 (horizontal)\n\nSo queen can attack g5 from:\n- f4: via Qh3-f4\n- h4: Qh3-h4\n- h5: Qh3-h5\n- h6: Qh3-h6\n- g4: Qh3-g4\n\nAll of these are in the valid move list.\n\nSo checking moves:\n- Q: h3-f4 → Qf4+\n- Q: h3-g4 → Qg4+\n- Q: h3-h4 → Qh4+\n- Q: h3-h5 → Qh5+\n- Q: h3-h6 → Qh6+\n\nNow, which one is best?\n\nWe want to **restrict the White king’s escape** and aim for **checkmate**.\n\nAfter any check, White king flees.\n\nBut notice: if we play **Q: h3-h6**, then:\n- Qh6+ \n- King on g5 must move to: g6, g4, h5, h4, f5, f4, f6, h6 (occupied), g7, etc.\n- But g6 is attacked by