king’s escape.\n- **Queen moves**:\n  - **Q: f5-e4, Q: f5-f4, Q: f5-g4, Q: f5-h3**: These bring the queen closer to the White king but may allow escape or even risk stalemate if not careful.\n  - **Q: f5-f8, Q: f5-h7, Q: f5-g6**: These aim to deliver checks or control key escape squares.\n    - **Q: f5-g6+** would attack g6 directly — but g6 is occupied by the White king (KWF1), so this is a capture: Qxg6.\n    - However, **Q: f5-g6** is listed as a valid move → implies it\'s legal.\n    - If the queen captures on g6, that’s checkmate? Let\'s verify:\n        - After Qxg6, White king is on g6, captured → removed. But wait: if Black queen moves to g6, and White king is on g6, then yes — queen captures king? That ends the game.\n        - But in chess, you don\'t "capture" the king — you checkmate it. So any move that places the opponent\'s king under attack with no legal escape is checkmate.\n        - Is Qf5-g6 a legal move? Only if it results in check or checkmate.\n        - Current position: White king on g6, Black queen on f5. Can the queen move to g6?\n            - Yes — same diagonal (f5 to g6 is one square diagonally up-right).\n            - So Qf5 → g6 attacks g6, where the White king is.\n            - After Qg6, the White king is in check.\n            - Can the White king escape? From g6, possible squares: f6, f7, f8, g7, g8, h6, h7, h8.\n            - Are any of these safe?\n                - f6: attacked by Black queen on g6? No — queen on g6 controls g-file, 6th rank, and diagonals. f6 is one diagonal away (g6→f5… not f6). Wait: g6 to f6 is horizontal — yes, queen controls entire g-file and 6th rank? No:\n                    - Queen on g6 controls:\n                        - Entire g-file\n                        - Entire 6th rank\n                        - Diagonals: a0-h7 diagonal and a12-h(-6)? Specifically: f5, e4, d3, c2, b1 and h7, etc.\n                    - So:\n                        - f6: not on same file/rank/diagonal as g6? It’s adjacent