tacking g6 along the 8th rank? No — f8 to g6 is not same rank or file.\n    - f8 controls f-file and 8th rank and diagonals.\n    - From f8, queen attacks: f6? yes (same file), f7, f6, etc. — but g6? no — not same file, not same rank, not diagonal (f8–g6 is knight move). So no.\n- **Q: f5-e6**: e6 is adjacent to g6? No. e6 to g6 = two files apart. Not attacking.\n- **Q: f5-h7**: h7 — same diagonal as g6? g6 to h7 is one square up-right → yes, diagonal. So queen on h7 attacks g6.\n    - After Qh7+, king must move.\n    - Escape squares: f6, f7, f8, g7, h6, h8\n    - Check if attacked:\n        - f6: not attacked by Qh7\n        - f7: h7–f7? two left — same rank → yes → attacked\n        - f8: h7–f8? two left, one up → diagonal → yes → attacked\n        - g7: down one from h7 → same file → attacked\n        - h6: down one → same file → attacked\n        - h8: up one → same file → attacked\n        - f6: not on same line — king can go there\n    - So king escapes to f6.\n    - Then game continues — not decisive.\n- Better idea: **Cut off the king with queen on the 7th or 8th rank**.\n- **Q: f5-f7** — queen moves to f7, which is adjacent to g6.\n    - Controls: entire f-file, 7th rank, and diagonals.\n    - Attacks g6? f7 to g6 = one square down-right → diagonal → yes.\n    - So Qf7+ checks the king.\n    - Escape squares: f6, f7 (occupied), f8, g7, h7, h6, h8\n        - f6: same file → attacked by queen\n        - f8: same file → attacked\n        - g7: same rank → attacked\n        - h7: same rank → attacked\n        - h6: not on same line → not attacked → king can go there\n        - h8: not attacked\n        - But king can move to h6 or h8? From g6 to h6: one right → yes. h6 not attacked by queen on f7? f7 to h6 = two right, one down → knight move → not attacked.\n    - So h6 is safe.\n    - Not mate.\n- Instead, **use king to support mate**.\n- Best idea: Bring Black king closer to join the attack.\n- **K: c4-d5** centralizes and cuts off White king’s flight to d-file.\n- Or **K: c4-c5** — similar.\n- But more direct: **Q: f5-e5** — cuts off 5th rank.\n    - Not immediate threat.\n- Wait: **Q: f5-c8** — long diagonal from c8 to g6? c8 to g6: +4 files, -2 ranks → not diagonal.\n- Actually, **Q: f5-h5** — h5 controls h-file and 5th rank and diagonals.\n    - h5 to g6 = one square down-left → diagonal → yes, attacks g6.\n    - So Qh5+ checks king.\n    - Escape squares: f6, f7, f8, g7, h7, h6\n        - f6: not attacked\n        - f7: h5–f7 = two left, two up → diagonal → yes → attacked\n        - f8: three left, three up → diagonal → yes → attacked\n        - g7: h5–g7 = one left, two up → knight move → not attacked\n        - h7: two up → same file → attacked\n        - h6: one up → same file → attacked\n    - So king can go to f6 or g7.\n    - Still escapes.\n- None of the queen checks give immediate mate.\n- So instead of checking, **restrict first**.\n- Best move: **Q: f5-f8** — not for attack, but to control the 8th rank and prevent king from going to f8/h8 later.\n    - But f8 is far.\n- Alternatively, **Q: f5-d7** — d7 controls d-file, 7th rank, and diagonals.\n    - d7 to g6 = three right, one down → not straight → no.\n- Wait: **Q: f5-e6** — e6 is one square left-down from f5.\n    - e6 to g6 = two right → same rank → yes, queen on e6 controls g6 via 6th rank.\n    - So Qe6 attacks g6.\n    - Is it check? Yes.\n    - Escape squares:\n        - f6: same rank → attacked\n        - f7: e6–f7 = one right, one up → diagonal → yes → attacked\n        - f8: two up, one right → knight move → not attacked\n        - g7: one up → same file → attacked\n        - h6: two right → same rank → attacked\n        - h7: one up, two right → knight move → not attacked\n        - h8: etc.\n    - So king can go to f8 or h7.\n    - Still escapes.\n- Realization: Without blocking or zugzwang, hard to mate.\n- But Black has a **king on c4**, can we bring it closer?\n- Key idea: **Invade with king**. The White king is on g6, very far from Black\'s forces. Black should use king to occupy the center and promote the pawn or support queen.\n\nBut pawn is on b5 — can be pushed.\n\nBut more urgent: **The White king is exposed. Can we trap it?**\n\nWait — what happens after **Q: f5-g6**?\n\n- Qxg6 — queen captures on g6, giving check.\n- As analyzed, king can go to f8 (only safe square?).\n\nBut let\'s recheck:\n\nAfter Qg6, White king on g6 is in check.\n\nPossible moves:\n\n- f6: attacked by queen (same rank)\n- f7: attacked (diagonal: g6–f7? no, queen is on g6 now — wait! After queen moves to g6, she replaces the king? No — the king is still on g6.\n\nYou cannot move your piece to a square occupied by your own king — but here, Black queen moving to g6 where White king is — that’s a capture.\n\nIn chess, you can capture an enemy piece (but not king — because king is never actually captured). But when you attack the king with no escape, it\'s checkmate.\n\nSo: **Black queen moves to g6, attacking the White king**.\n\nIs this check? Yes.\n\nCan the White king move to any safe square?\n\nFrom g6:\n\n- f6: queen on g6 controls g-file and 6th rank → f6 is on 6th rank → attacked\n- f7: g6 to f7 = one left, one up → diagonal → queen controls that diagonal → attacked\n- f8: two up, one left → knight move → not attacked by queen\n- g7: up one → same file → attacked\n- g8: further up → same file → attacked\n- h6: right one → same rank → attacked\n- h7: up-right → diagonal → attacked\n- h8: two up, one right → knight move → not attacked\n\nSo only **f8** and **h8** are not attacked.\n\nBut can the king move to them?\n\n- f8: from g6 to f8 = one file left, two ranks up → knight move → not allowed. King moves one square.\n- So f8 is not reachable.\n\nSimilarly, h8: g6 to h8 = one file right, two ranks up → knight move → not reachable.\n\nTherefore, the only possible escape squares are the eight surrounding squares: f6, f7, f8 (no), g7, h7, h6, h8 (no), f8 (no) — only adjacent ones.\n\nAdjacent: f6, f7, g7, h7, h6.\n\nAll of these are attacked by the queen on g6:\n\n- f6: same rank → attacked\n- f7: diagonal (g6–f7) → yes, same diagonal (a1–h8 direction) → attacked\n- g7: same file → attacked\n- h7: diagonal → g6–h7 → yes → attacked\n- h6: same rank → attacked\n\nTherefore, **every legal move by the White king is either off-board or to a square attacked by the queen**.\n\nThus, **Q: f5-g6 is checkmate**.\n\nWait — is that correct?\n\nQueen on g6 delivers check, and all escape squares are attacked → yes → **checkmate**.\n\nBut is g6 a legal destination?\n\nYes — Black queen on f5 can move diagonally to g6 (one square up-right).\n\nAnd White king is on g6 — so capturing it? Not literally, but the move places the king in check with no escape → game over.\n\nTherefore, **Q: f5-g6** is checkmate.\n\nBut why is it in the valid moves list? Because the engine allows it — and it should be.\n\nSo this is the winning move.\n\nReason: Moving the queen to g6 delivers checkmate. The White king has no legal moves, as all adjacent squares are controlled by the Black queen. This ends the game immediately.\n\nAnswer: Q: f5-g6'}
