4, so from b3 I can capture the pawn if it goes b5-b4? No — pawns move forward, not sideways. The pawn on b5 moves to b4 (forward), and then from b3, I can play K:b3xb4.\n\nWait: current pawn is on b5. If it moves b5-b4, then it's on b4. From c4, I can go c4-b4 or c4-c3 or c4-d4, etc.\n\nBut from c4, K: c4-b3 is one square diagonally (left-down), which is legal.\n\nLet’s list the valid moves again:\n- P: b5-b4 — White’s pawn advances\n- K: c4-b3 — Black king to b3\n- K: c4-b4 — to b4\n- K: c4-c3 — down\n- K: c4-c5 — up\n- K: c4-d3 — right-down\n- K: c4-d4 — right\n- K: c4-d5 — right-up\n\nAll of these are king moves except the pawn move.\n\nMy best plan: approach the pawn to prevent its advance or capture it.\n\nKey idea: If I play K: c4-b4, I occupy b4, so when White plays b5-b4, it's impossible — the square is occupied. But pawns cannot capture straight ahead — only diagonally. So if I'm on b4, and pawn is on b5, it cannot move to b4 because it's blocked. That’s key.\n\nSo by moving K: c4-b4, I **block** the pawn’s advance. The pawn is frozen unless it can capture diagonally — but there are no pieces on a6 or c6. So it cannot move at all after b4 is occupied.\n\nTherefore, K: c4-b4 **completely stops the pawn**, leading to a draw or even a loss for White if no other moves exist. But wait — in this position, after I move, White will have to move their king. Their king is on g6, far away. They’ll try to support the pawn, but it’s too far.\n\nBy playing K: c4-b4, I control b4, blocking b5-b4. Then if White moves their king, I can eventually capture the pawn if it ever advances (but it can’t). Actually, the pawn is permanently blocked. So the game could become a draw if neither side can make progress.\n\nBut what if instead I prepare to capture?\n\nSuppose I play K: c4-b3 — now I’m closer to b2, but the pawn is on b5. If it goes b5-b4, I can go K: b3-b4 next move and capture it? No — I can only capture if it lands on b4, but after b5-b4, the pawn is on b4, and I can recapture with K: b3xb4 — yes, since it's adjacent.\n\nBut if I go to b3 now, White plays b5-b4, then I can take it next move.\n\nBut if I go to b4 now, I block it immediately.\n\nIs there a difference?\n\nYes: if I go K: c4-b4, the pawn cannot move at all. It's completely blocked. White must use king moves to try to dislodge me, but their king is very far. Meanwhile, I can keep the king on b4 or maneuver as needed.\n\nIf I go K: c4-b3, White can still play b5-b4 (not capturing), and then the pawn is on b4, and I can take it with K: b3-b4 — but that’s on my next move. So same result, just one move later.\n\nBut by going to b3 first, I allow the pawn to advance to b4, which might be dangerous if there were other factors — but there aren't.\n\nHowever, occupying b4 immediately is better because:\n- It freezes the pawn permanently.\n- White gains no tempo.\n- It forces White to bring the king all the way from g6 to try to free the pawn, which is practically impossible to achieve before being forced into a draw by lack of progress.\n\nAdditionally, if I go to b3, and White decides not to push the pawn immediately, they lose time — but still, the optimal plan is to block the pawn.\n\nBest move is to **occupy b4**, preventing any advance.\n\nThus, K: c4-b4 is superior to K: c4-b3.\n\nOther moves like c4-c3, d4, etc., move away and allow b5-b4 freely, which is worse.\n\nSo top candidates:\n- K: c4-b4 — blocks pawn, best\n- K: c4-b3 — prepares capture, good but allows pawn to advance one step\n\nSince K: c4-b4 is more direct and prevents any possibility of pawn advancement, it is the best.\n\nReason: Moving the king to b4 immediately blocks the white pawn on b5 from advancing further. This immobilizes White’s only active piece, and with their king far away, they cannot coordinate a breakthrough. This leads to a draw at worst, but may induce errors. Any other king move allows the pawn to advance, increasing risk.\n\nAnswer: K: c4-b4"}
