le to go to b6. Since it’s not in the list, we must assume those moves are not legal — possibly due to check or blocking? But no pieces are near.\n\nWait — crucial observation: the Black king is on b4, White king on h5. If I move my king, I risk allowing the White king to approach and stop any future pawn advance. But right now, with no pawn move available, I must use the king.\n\nLooking at the valid moves:\n- King can go to a3, a4, a5, b3, c3, c4, c5.\n\nI need to centralize or support the pawn's advance. The pawn on b5 needs protection and space to advance. Moving the king forward or toward the center will help lead the pawn to promotion.\n\nBest plan: Bring the Black king in front of the pawn to support its advance. Ideal squares would be c5, then c6, etc., supporting b7 and eventually b8.\n\nCurrently, c5 is within reach. From c5, the king supports b7 and prepares for the pawn push.\n\nAlso, the White king is on h5 — very far, but if I waste time, it may come to g6, f7, etc., to capture the pawn or block promotion.\n\nTherefore, the best move is to advance the king toward c5, centralizing and supporting the b-pawn.\n\nEvaluation:\n- K: b4-c5 — Best choice. Centralizes, supports pawn advance, prepares to escort pawn to promotion.\n- K: b4-a5 — Too far left, wastes time, doesn't support center or pawn.\n- K: b4-a4 / a3 — Drifting sideways/down, loses tempo.\n- K: b4-b3 / c3 — Retreats, poor in a race situation.\n\nReason: Advancing the king to c5 maximizes king activity and supports the b-pawn’s path to promotion. It gains central control and puts Black in the best position to push the pawn with minimal interference from the distant White king.\n\nAnswer: K: b4-c5"}
