\n1 |    |    |    |    |    |    |    |    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: b4-b3, K: c5-b5, K: c5-b6, K: c5-c4, K: c5-c6, K: c5-d4, K: c5-d5, K: c5-d6]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: The board is nearly empty, with only three pieces remaining: a White King on g6 (KWF1), a Black King on c5 (KBF1), and a Black Pawn on b4 (PBF1). It's Black's turn. The goal is to promote the pawn or maneuver the king to support its advance while preventing the white king from capturing it. The black pawn is one step away from queening if supported properly.\n\nEvaluation:\n- P: b4-b3 – Advances the pawn closer to promotion. This reduces the number of moves needed for promotion but doesn’t guarantee protection from the white king.\n- K: c5-b5 – Moves the black king left toward the pawn, increasing support.\n- K: c5-b6 – Approaches the pawn but overshoots slightly; less optimal than b5.\n- K: c5-c4 – Moves down, away from the action; weakens control over b3 and b4.\n- K: c5-c6 – Moves further from the pawn; poor coordination.\n- K: c5-d4 – Heads toward center but drifts from supporting b3 push.\n- K: c5-d5 – Centralizes slightly, but still distant from key pawns.\n- K: c5-d6 – Moves away from the pawn; worst king moves.\n\nBest options are P: b4-b3 (pawn push) and K: c5-b5 (king support). Pushing the pawn prematurely without king cover risks losing it after W: Kg6-f5 or similar, followed by capture on b3. However, the immediate pawn move gains tempo.\n\nBut since the pawn is already on b4 and moving to b3 brings it within one square of queening (b2), and the black king can follow up next move, advancing the pawn now limits the white king’s time to interfere.\n\nReason: Advancing the pawn immediately to b3 restricts the white king’s ability to approach in time. With correct follow-up (Kb5, then Kb4, etc.), Black will promote soon. Delaying with Kb5 first allows White to potentially position better (e.g., Kf5 or Ke5), so gaining space with the pawn now is critical.\n\nAnswer: P: b4-b3"}
