rong: it attacks g5, f6, e7, d8, and crucially, h8 — giving check! Wait: is h5 attacking h8? No — h5 is on the same file as h8, yes, but from e2 to h5 is diagonal? No — e2 to h5 is a diagonal move (e2→f3→g4→h5), so yes, it\'s valid for queen. But does it give check?\n\nLet’s verify: Queen on h5, White king on h8 — same file! So yes, Qh5 attacks h8 directly along the h-file. So **Q: e2-h5** gives check to the White king.\n\nAfter Q: e2-h5+, the White king must move. Only legal squares are g8 or g7 (h7 and h8 are attacked). But currently, only g8 is available (h7 is not attacked yet, but let\'s see).\n\nWait: King on h8. After Qh5, which squares does the queen attack?\n- Along the h-file: h1–h8 → so h8 is attacked → check!\n- Diagonally: from h5 to e8 → passes through g6, f7, e8 — so f7 and g6 are attacked, but not g8 or g7 directly.\n\nSo after Qh5+, Kh8 must move to g8 (only safe square — h7 is on same file and attacked, g7 is not attacked yet).\n\nThen next move, I can play Qh5-g6+ or bring king closer.\n\nBut even better: **Q: e2-h2** puts the queen on h2, controlling the entire h-file (h1–h8), so h8 is under attack — also check!\n\nSame effect: Qh2+ also forces Kh8–g8.\n\nBut Qh5 is better because it supports a quicker mate by covering g6 and f7, helping to box the king in the corner.\n\nEven better idea: **Q: e2-e8+** — this gives check on the e-file? No, e8 is not on the same rank or file as h8. e8 is three files away. So no — Qe8 does **not** give check.\n\nOnly queen moves that give check are those that attack h8: same rank (8th), same file (h), or same diagonal.\n\nDiagonals to h8: a1–h8 (main diagonal) — so b1, c2, d3, e4, f5, g6, h7 are on it. So only if queen moves to one of those squares.\n\ne2 to g4? g4 is not on a line to h8. e2 to h5? h5 is on the h-file — yes, same file as h8 → so yes, h5 attacks h8 vertically.\n\nSo **Q: e2-h5** gives check.\n\nSimilarly, **Q: e2-h2** also gives check (same h-file).\n\nBut h2 is further away — less central control.\