nce h5 attacks g6, but not g7 — wait no).\n- h5 attacks g6, f7, e8 diagonally, and h1–h8 vertically.\n\nSo from h5, queen attacks:\n- Vertically: h1 to h8 → so h7, h8 are attacked\n- Diagonally: h5-g6-f7-e8 → so g6, f7, e8\n\nSo after Qh5+, Kh8 must go to g8 (h7 is attacked).\n\nThen from g8, possible moves: g7, f7, f8, h8 (but h8 attacked), h7 (attacked), so only g7, f7, f8.\n\nBut f7 is attacked by queen (via diagonal), so only g7 and f8.\n\nSo king can go to g7 or f8.\n\nIf K goes to g7, then Qh5-g6+ wins.\n\nIf K goes to f8, then I can play Qh5-g6, threatening Qg6-g8#.\n\nSo very strong.\n\nCompare to Qh2: queen on h2 attacks h8 (check), king to g8.\n\nThen queen attacks h1–h8, so h7, h8 attacked.\n\nBut diagonally, h2-g3-f4-e5-d6-c7-b8 — so c7, b8, etc., not useful.\n\nSo less control over f7/g7.\n\nTherefore, **Q: e2-h5** is superior: it gives check and controls key diagonal squares.\n\nThus, it maximizes winning chances.\n\nReason: Q: e2-h5 delivers check to the White king on h8, forces it to g8, and simultaneously controls critical escape squares like h7, g6, and f7 via vertical and diagonal coverage. This accelerates the mating process by restricting the king’s mobility and setting up a quick follow-up threat like Qg6+ or Qg6 followed by mate. It is the most active and forcing move.\n\nAnswer: Q: e2-h5'}
